{"complete":true,"city":"Tampa, Florida","template_id":"global-first-24-hours","title":"Your First 24 Hours in Tampa, Dealt as Cards","description":"A first-day sequence that moves from books and Gilded Age rooms to the riverfront, Tampa’s layered regional history, Ybor City, and a night beneath a movie palace’s painted sky.","icon":"schedule","cards":[{"entity_name":"Oxford Exchange","title":"Oxford Exchange","subtitle":"Begin with a room built for browsing.","notes":"Role: the opening page. Browse the independent bookstore’s new releases, classics, signed editions, and children’s books before the day becomes more structured.","short_summary":"A book-lined start across Kennedy Boulevard from Plant Hall.","source_url":"https://bookstore.oxfordexchange.com/","source_title":"The Bookstore — Oxford Exchange","latitude":27.9449261,"longitude":-82.4646338,"place_id":"ChIJ11hsiYXEwogRjDBv39F04J8","maps_url":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/?q=place_id:ChIJ11hsiYXEwogRjDBv39F04J8"},{"entity_name":"Henry B. Plant Museum","title":"Henry B. Plant Museum","subtitle":"Read Tampa’s early tourism story through the surviving hotel.","notes":"Role: the historical prologue. The museum occupies part of the 1891 Tampa Bay Hotel and retains furnishings and artifacts collected by Henry and Margaret Plant.","short_summary":"Victorian objects connect railroad expansion, resort culture, and Tampa’s emergence onto a wider stage.","source_url":"https://www.ut.edu/about-utampa/henry-b-plant-museum","source_title":"Henry B. Plant Museum — The University of Tampa","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"Tampa Riverwalk","title":"Tampa Riverwalk","subtitle":"Let the Hillsborough River organize the middle of the day.","notes":"Role: the orientation walk. Use a central section of the multi-use path to connect downtown parks and museums rather than treating its full length as an assignment.","short_summary":"The 2.6-mile waterfront path links Armature Works and Sparkman Wharf through Tampa’s civic core.","source_url":"https://thetampariverwalk.com/who-we-are/frequent-questions.html","source_title":"Frequent Questions — Friends of the Riverwalk","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"Tampa Museum of Art","title":"Tampa Museum of Art","subtitle":"Make room for a visual change of scale.","notes":"Role: the art interval. Move between Greek and Roman antiquities and modern or contemporary work, noticing how the museum places those collections in one waterfront building.","short_summary":"Ancient objects and contemporary expressions share the museum’s downtown program.","source_url":"https://tampamuseum.org/about/","source_title":"About the Tampa Museum of Art","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"Tampa Bay History Center","title":"Tampa Bay History Center","subtitle":"Put the waterfront into a much longer timeline.","notes":"Role: the regional context stop. Follow exhibits from Indigenous history and European exploration through Tampa Bay’s development as an industrial and cultural center.","short_summary":"The galleries frame Tampa within 12,000 years of Florida history rather than beginning with the modern city.","source_url":"https://tampabayhistorycenter.org/about/","source_title":"About — Tampa Bay History Center","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"Columbia Cafe","title":"Columbia Cafe","subtitle":"Turn the history lesson into lunch.","notes":"Role: the midday table. Choose the Cuban sandwich to encounter the cafe’s account of how Spanish, Sicilian, Cuban, German, and Jewish ingredients converged in Tampa.","short_summary":"A Riverwalk lunch whose menu explicitly traces the city’s immigrant foodways.","source_url":"https://www.columbiarestaurant.com/columbia-cafe-at-the-tbhc","source_title":"Columbia Cafe at the Tampa Bay History Center — Columbia Restaurant","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"TECO Line Streetcar","title":"TECO Line Streetcar","subtitle":"Use the city’s connective line as part of the story.","notes":"Role: the district transition. Board the heritage streetcar for the passage from the downtown and Channel District side of the day toward Ybor City.","short_summary":"The electric heritage line connects Downtown Tampa, the Channel District, and Ybor City.","source_url":"https://www.tecolinestreetcar.org/howtoride.html","source_title":"How to Ride — TECO Line Streetcar","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"Ybor City Museum State Park","title":"Ybor City Museum State Park","subtitle":"Enter Ybor through the bakery that now holds its archive.","notes":"Role: the neighborhood key. Inside the historic Ferlita Bakery, use the self-guided exhibits to connect cigar manufacturing with the community that grew around it.","short_summary":"The museum traces Ybor City’s cultural history and cigar industry from a repurposed neighborhood bakery.","source_url":"https://www.floridastateparks.org/parks-and-trails/ybor-city-museum-state-park","source_title":"Ybor City Museum State Park — Florida State Parks","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"Columbia Restaurant — Ybor City","title":"Columbia Restaurant — Ybor City","subtitle":"Let dinner continue the neighborhood’s immigrant narrative.","notes":"Role: the evening meal. The Spanish-Cuban menu reflects five generations of family ownership; the tableside 1905 Salad ties Sicilian Romano cheese and Cuban garlic dressing to the restaurant’s Ybor origins.","short_summary":"Dinner connects family continuity, Spanish-Cuban cooking, and the layered food culture of Ybor City.","source_url":"https://www.columbiarestaurant.com/menu?menu=columbia-restaurant-lunch-menu","source_title":"Columbia Restaurant Lunch Menu — Columbia Restaurant","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"Tampa Theatre","title":"Tampa Theatre","subtitle":"End beneath an artificial night sky.","notes":"Role: the final curtain. Pair whatever film or program fits your date with a look at John Eberson’s atmospheric design: twinkling stars, tiled rooftops, statuary, and courtyard scenery.","short_summary":"The 1926 movie palace turns the last stop into both a screening and an encounter with preserved theatrical architecture.","source_url":"https://tampatheatre.org/about/history/","source_title":"History — Tampa Theatre","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null}],"warnings":["Check venue calendars before using this sequence: museum operating days and Tampa Theatre programming vary. No current hours, admission details, or travel-time claims are asserted here."]}
